<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Athis-sur-Orge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1305">1305</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-flemish-war-1297-1305">Franco-Flemish War (1297–1305)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Athis-sur-Orge was concluded in June 1305 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> after the French victory at Mons-en-Pévèle. It restored Count Robert of Béthune but imposed a heavy indemnity, acts of penance and the demolition of town fortifications. Disputes over its terms led to the transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douai">Douai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orchies">Orchies</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1312.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was signed at Athis-sur-Orge on 23 June 1305 between King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a> and Robert III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, and concluded the Franco-Flemish War, which had lasted from 1297 to 1305. It followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mons-en-pevele">Battle of Mons-en-Pévèle</a>. Although Philip had won that battle, his kingdom was too drained financially and militarily to take advantage of the victory, and he sought instead to make Flanders pay a heavy price for peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> kept its count but was to be held as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The Flemish were to pay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> of 400,000 pounds and a yearly penalty of 20,000, to furnish Philip with 600 knights, and to tear down the fortresses around their large cities. As an act of penance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> had to send 3,000 people on pilgrimage, 1,000 of whom were to travel overseas. Robert III, who had signed for the county, faced the impossible task of satisfying the French monarchy while keeping the Flemish cities from revolt, and it was only after 1309 that some of the reparations were actually paid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Because its financial terms were so severe, the treaty met wide disapproval in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. In June 1310 Pope Clement V, under pressure from Philip, declared that those who failed to fulfil its terms would be excommunicated. Tax collection brought riots in Aardenburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> in 1311. The burden fell hardest on the Flemish farmers and the middle class, and in 1323 the Peasant Revolt in Flanders broke out. It grew into a popular uprising that dominated Flemish politics for five years until it was crushed at the Battle of Cassel in 1328.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Athis-sur-Orge” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Athis-sur-Orge</b> of 1305 afterwards imposed heavy penalties on Flanders.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-18">18 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King Philip IV of France and Robert III of Flanders signed the <b>Treaty of Athis-sur-Orge</b>, ending the Franco-Flemish War after the French victory at Mons-en-Pévèle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-23">23 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
